Ansible Troubleshooting 🛠️

Today I learned about Ansible Troubleshooting, which is essential when Playbooks don’t behave as expected ⚠️
In real DevOps environments, failures are normal.
 Knowing how to debug them quickly is a superpower 💪

🔹 🔎 Verbose Mode
Ansible provides verbose output for debugging.
Use:
 -v → Basic details
 -vv → More details
 -vvv → Deep debugging
 -vvvv → Maximum verbosity

Example:
ansible-playbook playbook.yml -vvv

Verbose mode helps identify exactly where and why a task failed.

🔹 ❌ Common Errors
Some frequently seen issues:
• Module not found
 • Permission denied
 • Variable undefined
 • Inventory misconfiguration
Reading the error message carefully usually gives the solution 🎯

🔹 🔐 SSH Issues
Ansible uses SSH to connect to managed nodes.
Common SSH problems:
 • Wrong SSH key
 • Incorrect username
 • Port blocked
 • Host unreachable
Always verify manual SSH login first before debugging Playbooks.

🔹 📄 YAML Mistakes
YAML errors are very common and can break Playbooks instantly.
Typical mistakes:
 • Wrong indentation
 • Using tabs instead of spaces
 • Missing colon
 • Improper list formatting
Small formatting mistakes can cause big failures ⚠️

🔥 3-Line Takeaway
Verbose mode reveals hidden issues.
 Most errors come from SSH or YAML mistakes.
 Careful debugging saves deployment time.
